# Community-Driven Music

Discussion record — September 30, 2026
Owner: Ian Raymond
Status: Idea and platform exploration for later development. No platform selected,
software implementation authorized, or community launched during this discussion.

## Ian's purpose

Make audience participation in the music meaningful. Many projects invite people
to “be part of it” but offer only shallow interaction with no effect on the work.
Ian wants listeners' preferences and feedback to influence the music he releases,
including which recordings or versions become part of an album.

The project should also help attract visitors to Ian's website and get more
people listening to his music. It is an ongoing community around Ian's work,
not merely an audio hosting or professional mix-review service.

## Experiences Ian wants to support

- Post songs for listeners to review before deciding what to release.
- Post multiple versions of the same song and let visitors choose their favorite.
- Post a single version for feedback, without requiring a comparison.
- Post short excerpts, potentially only a few seconds, for focused review.
- Allow star ratings and optional comments.
- Consider comments and requests when developing the music and selecting album
  versions.
- Give participants some community recognition; some degree of gamification is
  desirable, but its form has not been chosen.

## Participation and identity

Ian strongly wants to avoid requiring people to create accounts. An email address
or another lightweight means of recognizing returning contributors may be
acceptable. No identity method has been selected.

Existing accounts on an external platform may reduce friction for people already
using it, but do not satisfy account-free participation for everyone. The account
requirement remains a tradeoff to evaluate, not a requirement Ian has waived.

Suggestions discussed, not adopted:

- Let people listen and vote first, then optionally provide an email address and
  public display name for recognition and follow-up.
- Use emailed sign-in links instead of passwords for returning participants.
- Offer song-specific acknowledgment or an album listener thank-you list.
- Use badges, roles, points, levels, or leaderboards supplied by an existing
  community platform.

Persistent recognition requires a way to recognize a returning person. Anonymous
voting is easier to repeat; verified email can reduce abuse but does not prove
one person per vote. These tradeoffs need evaluation in the chosen platform.

## Authorship and future collaboration

Ian wants feedback and requests without unintentionally creating additional
co-writers or ownership disputes over his existing songs.

Later, if participation grows, Ian may deliberately write and release some songs
under Creative Commons or into the public domain to enable an interactive,
collaborative writing process with fans.

Ordinary listening feedback and intentional collaborative songwriting therefore
need different expectations and submission terms. No license, rights agreement,
or legal conclusion was selected or established in this discussion. Choosing
Creative Commons or public-domain treatment for Ian's work does not by itself
settle the rights in other people's future contributions. This remains an issue
for proper review before accepting and using contributed lyrics or music.

## Build versus use an existing service

Ian initially proposed a new plugin for the music website, but explicitly said
he would consider an existing service if it could support the experience while
also attracting visitors or listeners.

Ian used Reddit as an example of a general community platform that could be
adapted to the purpose. He meant creating and running HIS OWN community. He did
not mean making someone else's subreddit the home of the project, and Reddit
was not a final platform choice.

Ian asked for an active comparison of alternatives, their advantages and
disadvantages, and existing services. Merely repeating his ideas back was not
helpful.

Critical correction from Ian: do not build custom software first to prove the
idea before trying an existing community solution. Evaluate and use an existing
solution first where practical. Custom development would be considered later if
actual experience reveals a worthwhile need that existing tools cannot meet.

No platform trial, build, purchase, or launch was selected or authorized.

## Community platform alternatives discussed

These are research leads, not endorsed or selected solutions. Features and plan
availability must be checked again before choosing a service. Discovery features
do not guarantee an audience or traffic to Ian's website.

### Facebook Groups

Create Ian's own group with listening posts, discussion, polls, and contributor
recognition. Public groups can be discovered within Facebook. Potentially low
friction for fans who already use Facebook.

Tradeoffs: Facebook identity is required for participation; community activity
primarily happens on Facebook; limited control over audio comparison and the
listener experience. Driving listeners onward to Ian's site needs a reason.

Sources:
- https://about.fb.com/news/2020/10/privacy-matters-the-new-public-groups/
- https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/find-community-forum-dedicated-app-facebook-groups/amp/

### Reddit — Ian's own subreddit

Public discussion, polls, persistent usernames, and recurring listening rounds.
Could preserve the conversation around each musical decision. Ian controls the
community's organization and moderation within Reddit's rules.

Tradeoffs: voting and comments require Reddit accounts; audio comparison is not
a purpose-built listening interface; creating a subreddit does not automatically
bring an audience. Traffic and relationships remain substantially on Reddit.

Sources:
- https://redditinc.com/news/introducing-reddit-polls-an-all-new-post-type
- https://support.reddithelp.com/hc/en-us/articles/360043033532-How-do-I-embed-a-Reddit-post-or-comment-in-an-article-or-other-publication

### Discord

Ian's own server with song discussions, polls, contributor roles, and live
listening sessions. A possible fit for frequent interaction among active fans.

Tradeoffs: account and joining/navigation friction for casual listeners; native
polls are not anonymous. Built-in Server Discovery has eligibility requirements,
including at least 1,000 members and a server age of eight weeks according to
the documentation consulted. It should not be treated as immediate discovery
for a new community.

Sources:
- https://support.discord.com/hc/en-us/articles/22163184112407-Polls-FAQ
- https://support.discord.com/hc/en-us/articles/360030843331-Enabling-Server-Discovery
- https://support.discord.com/hc/en-us/articles/11074987197975-Community-Onboarding-FAQ

### Skool

An owned group within Skool with community discussion, polls, points, levels,
and leaderboards. Has a discovery directory with activity eligibility and ranking
criteria. A candidate for combining visible recognition with platform discovery.

Tradeoffs: members need accounts; host costs and plan details need checking;
the platform may be unfamiliar to fans. Its documented points system rewards
likes on posts/comments/replies, which may not correspond to careful listening
or useful musical feedback.

Sources:
- https://help.skool.com/article/183-how-do-points-and-level-work
- https://help.skool.com/article/153-discovery-faqs

### Circle

A branded hosted community with separate spaces, posts, polls, events, and
gamification. Could organize songs and their discussions without building the
whole community system.

Tradeoffs: paid service and member signup. Treat it primarily as a home for an
audience Ian attracts; research did not establish it as a source of new music
listeners. Exact audio hosting/embedding and comparison behavior need checking.

Sources:
- https://help.circle.so/p/posts/post-spaces/create-polls
- https://circle.so/gamification
- https://help.circle.so/p/audience/gamification/get-to-know-gamification

### Mighty Networks

A hosted fan-community option with posts, polls, questions, discussions,
livestreams, events, and recognition systems. Could suit a broader fan club
with recurring listening events.

Tradeoffs: paid hosting, membership friction, and potentially more platform than
the initial concept needs. Audience acquisition and the exact audio workflow
remain to be evaluated.

Source:
- https://www.mightynetworks.com/community

### Discourse

An independent forum, potentially on a subdomain of Ian's website. One topic
could retain a song's versions, polls, replies, decisions, and badges in a
searchable archive. Hosted and self-hosted approaches exist.

Tradeoffs: hosting/administration responsibility; standard participation uses
accounts. Email login links can reduce password friction but are not equivalent
to account-free participation. No built-in audience should be assumed.

Sources:
- https://www.discourse.org/features
- https://meta.discourse.org/t/direct-url-for-the-email-me-a-login-link-feature/343627

### Existing WordPress community tools: BuddyBoss / wpForo

Could add community functions to Ian's existing website without writing a whole
community platform. BuddyBoss offers social-community features and gamification.
wpForo offers forum discussions, reputation badges, and a separate polls add-on.

Tradeoffs: plugin maintenance, configuration, potential licensing costs, and
integration work. Their member-recognition systems generally depend on user
identities. Account-free voting must be verified; it was not established as
available out of the box. These tools do not automatically bring an audience.

Sources:
- https://buddyboss.com/blog/introducing-gamification/
- https://wpforo.com/docs/wpforo-v3/settings/member-rating/
- https://gvectors.com/product/wpforo-polls/
- https://wordpress.org/plugins/wpforo/

## Audio-specific tools encountered in research

These may provide parts of the experience, but Ian correctly pointed out that a
music service alone is not necessarily a community solution for this concept.

- MixTester advertises multiple versions, instant A/B switching, favorite-mix
  voting, timestamped comments, blind playback, and loudness matching. It could
  supply an audio comparison component. Research did not establish audience
  discovery, community recognition, or the exact reviewer-account requirements.
  https://mixtester.com/
- SoundCloud supports embedded audio, timestamped comments, public tracks, and
  private sharing links. It could host recordings, but was not established as
  a complete version-voting and community-impact system.
  https://help.soundcloud.com/hc/en-us/articles/115003566008-Comments
  https://help.soundcloud.com/hc/en-us/articles/115003453587
- BandLab / ReverbNation Crowd Review appeared in research as a way to obtain
  ratings and feedback. The article consulted was an older rollout announcement;
  current availability was not verified. Paid or panel-based song review is a
  different purpose from an ongoing community of Ian's fans.
  https://blog.bandlab.com/introducing-crowd-review/
- SoundRoster advertises fan voting and music discovery, but asks users to sign
  up to vote and participate. It was not established as a place for Ian to run
  his own development community or compare versions of his songs.
  https://soundroster.com/for-artists

## Possible operating practices — suggestions only

These ideas were proposed by the assistant; Ian did not adopt them as a final
design or authorize implementing them.

- Run a specific listening question for each round, such as a preference between
  two performances, while retaining single-song ratings and snippet reviews.
- Offer “no preference” and “neither” where useful rather than forcing a choice.
- For mix comparisons, use comparable loudness, neutral version labels, and
  optional hidden results until a listener answers.
- Keep feedback attached to the exact version heard, rather than replacing
  audio underneath old comments.
- Close rounds with a visible decision and an explanation of how feedback
  affected the recording or release.
- Recognize helpful participation without rewarding agreement with Ian or
  voting for the eventual winner.
- Consider song-specific acknowledgments and an optional album thank-you list.
- Observe repeat participation and useful feedback, not only total vote counts.
- Consider a combination of a hosted community and existing website audio,
  without requiring a new plugin to try the concept.

Josephine's original and gentle mouth-cleanup listening versions were suggested
as possible comparison material. Ian did not authorize posting them publicly,
select them for a community trial, or approve either as the official album
version. Their earlier review-only status remains in effect.

## Questions still open

1. Which platform best matches Ian's actual prospective listeners and their
   existing habits?
2. Which options offer credible discovery of new listeners, and which primarily
   require Ian to bring an audience?
3. Is participation with an existing social account acceptable, or is fully
   account-free participation essential?
4. How easily can listeners hear a whole song, a short excerpt, and multiple
   versions on each platform, especially on mobile?
5. How should recognition work, and how much public identity should be optional?
6. Should ratings and comments be public immediately, moderated first, or shown
   only after a round closes?
7. What feedback and submission terms will protect ordinary review activity,
   and what separate terms would govern intentional songwriting collaboration?
8. What hosting or subscription budget is acceptable?
9. What specific limitation, observed in real use, would justify custom software?

## Handoff instructions

Continue from Ian's concept and constraints, not from an assumed platform choice.
Provide concrete alternatives and tradeoffs. Do not simply paraphrase his ideas,
focus only on the last example he mentions, or treat assistant suggestions as
requirements Ian approved.

The next useful work is evaluating existing solutions against this particular
experience. Building a custom plugin first to validate demand was explicitly
rejected. This file records the discussion for later work; it is not an
implementation specification or permission to publish recordings.
